# /bootstrap — one-time project init

Capture durable codebase context to `PROJECT.md` so every downstream smriti skill (`/office-hours`, `/plan-eng-review`, etc.) starts with the same picture of the project.

## Hard gate

If `PROJECT.md` already exists at the repo root **and** the user did not explicitly pass `--force`, abort immediately with:

> Already bootstrapped. Read `PROJECT.md` for context. Use `/bootstrap --force` to redo.

Do not proceed past this gate. Do not ask any questions.

## Phase 1: Pre-fill from existing signals

Silently read these (do not echo contents to the user):

```bash
[ -f package.json ] && head -60 package.json
[ -f pyproject.toml ] && head -40 pyproject.toml
[ -f Cargo.toml ] && head -30 Cargo.toml
[ -f go.mod ] && head -10 go.mod
[ -f README.md ] && head -80 README.md
git log --oneline -30 2>/dev/null
git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null
ls vercel.json fly.toml railway.toml render.yaml Dockerfile 2>/dev/null
```

Form a draft picture:
- **Project name** — from `package.json#name`, `pyproject.toml#project.name`, or the repo's directory name as fallback
- **Tech stack** — inferred from deps + file extensions + config files
- **Likely deploy target** — `vercel.json` → Vercel, `fly.toml` → Fly, `railway.toml` → Railway, `Dockerfile` alone → self-hosted, none → not yet
- **Recency / activity** — number of commits in last 30 days (proxy for how active this is)

Use this draft to pre-fill defaults in Phase 2 — the user should not have to retype things you already know.

## Phase 2: Single bundled AskUserQuestion

Ask **one** AskUserQuestion covering all seven fields. Pre-fill from Phase 1 wherever possible. Mark required vs. optional clearly.

| # | Field | Options |
|---|-------|---------|
| 1 | **Project type** | business / side project / open source / learning / client work |
| 2 | **Target user (one sentence)** | free text |
| 3 | **Monetization** (only if business) | subscription / one-time / freemium / ads / enterprise / not yet |
| 4 | **Lean** | `senior` (rigor; exhaustive failure-mode review) OR `prototype` (speed; ship rough) |
| 5 | **Tech stack** | confirm pre-filled list, edit as needed |
| 6 | **Deploy target** | Vercel / Fly / Railway / Render / self-hosted / not yet / other |
| 7 | **Design priority** | high (UI is the product) / medium (UI matters but isn't primary) / low (CLI / lib / backend) |

## Phase 3: Persist

After the user answers:

**3a. Persist `lean` globally** (so `/plan-eng-review` and others can read it):

```bash
smriti-config set lean <senior|prototype>
```

**3b. Write `PROJECT.md` to the repo root.** Synthesize the Overview — don't just echo the user's Phase 2 answers verbatim. Write a tight paragraph that captures what this project is, who it's for, and what success looks like.

```markdown
# Project: <name>

## Overview
<one or two paragraphs: what this project is, who it's for, what success looks like>

## Users
<target user from Phase 2, expanded to a few sentences if useful>

## Lean
**<senior | prototype>.** <one-line rationale: why this lean, what tradeoffs the user accepted>

## Tech Stack
- <bullet list>

## Deploy
<deploy target + any specifics>

## Design Priority
**<high | medium | low>.** <one-line rationale>

## Decisions Log
- <YYYY-MM-DD> Bootstrapped via /bootstrap.
```

**3c. Log a project-level architecture learning** at high confidence:

```bash
smriti-learnings-log \
  --skill bootstrap \
  --type architecture \
  --key project-overview \
  --insight "<one-sentence summary: what + who + lean>" \
  --confidence 9 \
  --source user-stated
```

## Phase 4: Handoff

- If `design priority = high` AND `DESIGN.md` does NOT exist:
  > "Bootstrapped. Recommend `/design-consultation` next to lock in your design system before building."
- Otherwise:
  > "Bootstrapped. `PROJECT.md` written. Use `/office-hours` to brainstorm a feature, or just describe what you want to build."

## Done

State concisely: *"Bootstrapped. PROJECT.md written. Future skills in this repo will read it for context."*

